---
import BlogPost from '../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How can I speed read a PDF online without uploading it?',
    a: 'Open BriskRead, choose Upload file, and select your PDF. PDF.js extracts the text locally inside your browser so your file never leaves your device.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can BriskRead speed read scanned image PDFs?',
    a: 'Yes. For scanned pages or screenshots that have no selectable text layer, use the on-device Image OCR tab powered by Tesseract.js.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a page or word limit for PDFs?',
    a: 'No. BriskRead has no page count or word count cap, so you can load full textbooks, reports, and EPUB books for free.',
  },
];
---

<BlogPost slug="speed-read-pdf-online" faqs={faqs} related={['how-to-speed-read-online', 'free-online-speed-reader', 'free-speechify-alternatives']}>
  <p class="lede">
    Most “PDF to speech” or “PDF speed reader” tools upload your file. A better default: parse the PDF in the browser, then run <a href="/rsvp-reader">RSVP</a> or Focus. That is the <a href="/pdf-to-speech">PDF workflow BriskRead is built for</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Step-by-step on BriskRead</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the <a href="/">online speed reader</a></li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Upload file</strong> → PDF, EPUB, or TXT</li>
    <li>Wait for local parsing (no account required for free use)</li>
    <li>Start in Flash for known material, or Focus for dense chapters</li>
    <li>Optional: enable <strong>Audio Sync</strong> for dual-channel reading</li>
    <li>Save to library if you want to resume later</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    BriskRead is free with no length caps, so full books and long reports work the same as short articles; ads support the service.
  </p>

  <h2>Scanned PDFs</h2>
  <p>
    If the file is image-only, use <strong>Image OCR</strong> (or OCR fallback when text extraction is empty). OCR is slower but still client-side in BriskRead.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Privacy summary: document bytes for PDF/EPUB/OCR stay in your browser. Optional URL extract and AI features only run when you use them (AI uses your own Gemini key). See the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> or test a sample in the <a href="/readability-checker">readability checker</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BlogPost>
